      <h2>What the drills model</h2>
      <p>
        Both guided practice and challenges use the real 600 ms game tick.
        Guided mode adds explanations without changing the rhythm. Prayers,
        movement, and items use mouse clicks. Configurable function keys switch
        inventory and prayer tabs only. Blob read phases vary by exercise, with
        a six-tick cycle and an attack three ticks after the read. Two-tick
        drills deliberately distinguish aligned and misaligned scan phases. With
        no protection prayer, the exercise chooses a reproducible style; that is
        a practice choice, not a prediction of the game. Magers and rangers use
        four-tick attacks. Stack exercises begin with an explicitly labelled
        one- or two-tick offset.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rhythm and alternation lessons score their requested pattern.
        Conservation also checks one off–on pair between ticks; simply holding
        prayer does not pass. Reverse-flick and melee–blob exercises score the
        requested mitigation priorities, while separately reporting attacks left
        unprotected. Blob lessons score both controlled reads and protected
        attacks. Other lessons score attack checks and, where applicable,
        movement targets. A score is correct checks divided by total checks; it
        does not measure damage, prayer drain, or real-world latency.
      </p>
      <p>
        Supply exercises model finite stock and a three-tick cooldown for
        standard food or potions, based on{' '}
        <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Food/Fast_foods">
          food timing
        </a>{' '}
        and{' '}
        <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Potions">potion timing</a>.
        The brew exercise uses a fixed three-brew/one-restore sequence as a
        dose-tracking task. It does not simulate HP, stat levels, boosts, or the
        attack delay caused by eating. Supplies do not switch prayers or stop
        enemy attacks.
      </p>
      <p>
        The marked tile exercise is an invented coordination drill, not an
        Inferno floor hazard. Click movement places the player directly on the
        chosen tile, without pathfinding. These exercises do not model combat
        damage, gear, melee range or full wave solving. Jad exercises use
        labelled cues and delayed prayer checks, not exact game animations. The
        shoot–step drill models a two-tick weapon rhythm, not Zuk’s shield. The
        course covers encounter decisions and links full Jad and Zuk practice
        externally. Use the linked guides and simulator to continue learning.
      </p>
      <h2>Your progress</h2>
      <p>
        Knowledge-check answers, completed runs and scores are saved in this
        browser’s local storage. Guided or interrupted runs count as practice.
        Two uninterrupted challenges at 90% or more earn lesson mastery. Mastery
        is a learning milestone, not a guarantee of an Inferno completion.
        Hidden tabs and delayed browser timers pause the drill. You can delete
        local scores on the Your progress page.
      </p>
      <p>
        Fonts are loaded from Google Fonts; otherwise the drills and game images
        run locally after the site has loaded. External resources open only when
        you follow their links.
      </p>
